    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_a1a9b2a3",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "quentin_tarantino",
      "label": "Quentin Tarantino",
      "description": "The man in the image is widely recognized as Quentin Tarantino, the acclaimed American filmmaker known for his distinctive style, violent yet stylized narratives, and frequent use of pop culture references. His public persona often includes a confrontational or dismissive demeanor, which aligns with the aggressive pointing gesture and frustrated expression in the image. His association with memes and internet culture, particularly those referencing his films or style, is a real-world context that helps interpret the meme’s self-referential humor about being reported for memes.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bab643f8"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_09d0dc84",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "venice_film_festival",
      "label": "Venice International Film Festival",
      "description": "The red backdrop with the logo 'MOSTRA INTERNAZIONALE D'ARTE CINEMATOGRAFICA' and the number '67' indicates the image was taken at the 67th Venice International Film Festival in 2010. This event is a prestigious international film festival, and the context of a celebrity attending such an event adds to the meme’s humor by juxtaposing a highbrow cultural setting with the lowbrow, internet-driven frustration about meme reporting. The festival’s branding is a real-world entity that provides context for the setting.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bab643f8"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7932a435",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "social_media_reporting",
      "label": "Social Media Content Reporting",
      "description": "The text 'i get reported all the fuckin time' refers to the real-world phenomenon of content moderation on social media platforms, where users can report posts (including memes) for violating community guidelines. This system is often criticized for being inconsistent or overly aggressive, and the meme humorously expresses the frustration of creators who feel their content is unfairly flagged. This concept is not visible in the image but is essential to understanding the meme’s message.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bab643f8"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_4ede2e5f",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "internet_meme_culture",
      "label": "Internet Meme Culture",
      "description": "The meme references the broader cultural phenomenon of internet memes — humorous, often viral images or videos that spread rapidly online. The frustration expressed in the text is rooted in the reality that meme creators frequently face backlash or moderation for their content, even when it is popular or well-intentioned. This cultural context is necessary to understand the self-referential nature of the meme.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bab643f8"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_3ecabfbf",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "frustration",
      "label": "frustrated emotion",
      "description": "The man's facial expression, combined with his aggressive pointing gesture, conveys a strong sense of frustration. His furrowed brow, narrowed eyes, and dismissive posture indicate irritation and annoyance, amplified by the text overlay that explicitly states he is frequently reported for his memes.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bab643f8"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_3de07125",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_1",
      "label": "The man is mapped as the frustrated meme creator",
      "description": "The text 'i don't need you to tell me how fuckin good my memes are...' and 'i get reported all the fuckin time' explicitly refer to the speaker's identity as the creator of memes who is being reported. The visual material of the man pointing aggressively with a frustrated expression is mapped onto this textual self-reference. The man's demeanor and gesture visually embody the frustration and defensiveness described in the text, creating an analogical mapping where the person in the image is the 'me' in the text.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bab643f8"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_0774a2d3",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the meme creator (the man)",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the man in the plaid shirt, who is visually and textually represented as the 'me' in the meme. The text explicitly uses 'me' ('i don't need you to tell me...') and 'my memes', directly identifying the man as the speaker and the subject of the frustration. The user sees themselves as the meme creator being reported, and the visual of the man pointing aggressively embodies this self-referential projection. The user is projecting their own experience of being reported onto this visual and textual representation.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bab643f8"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_bfebd9ac",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_2",
      "label": "User projected onto the target of the frustration (the reporting system or audience)",
      "description": "The user is also projected onto the implied 'you' in the text ('i don't need you to tell me...'). The user sees themselves as the meme creator, and the 'you' represents the audience, moderators, or social media platforms that are reporting their content. The aggressive pointing gesture of the man visually directs the blame or frustration towards this 'you'. The user is projecting their own frustration at the reporting system onto the visual of the man pointing, which symbolizes the external force causing the reports.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bab643f8"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
